description:
What is awkward: pkix-aia/src/lib.rs:289-314 (label_for) and 321-344 (kind_for) implement the same 20-variant table in opposite directions. Adding or removing a variant requires editing both arms; the build does not check that they stay paired.

Suggested rewrite: a macro_rules! that emits both directions from a single list, OR a single const VARIANTS: &[(ErrorKind, &str)] table that both functions iterate.

Example shape (illustrative, not a strict prescription):

const VARIANTS: &[(ErrorKind, &str)] = &[
    (ErrorKind::NotFound, "NotFound"),
    // ... 19 more
];
fn label_for(k: ErrorKind) -> &'static str {
    VARIANTS.iter().find(|(kk, _)| *kk == k).map(|(_, s)| *s).unwrap_or("Other")
}
fn kind_for(s: &str) -> ErrorKind {
    VARIANTS.iter().find(|(_, ss)| *ss == s).map(|(k, _)| *k).unwrap_or(ErrorKind::Other)
}

The cost in lookup performance (20-element linear scan vs match jump) is irrelevant — this code runs at most once per serde call on an error path.

Tagged 'opinion': the duplication is readable and grep-friendly as-is, so this is on the line between elegance and bikeshedding. Calling it explicitly. The find-and-fix would be more compelling if PKIX-o3op.1 is resolved by extending the table (option a) — at 35+ variants, the duplication becomes a real maintenance issue.
